00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.960
We often hear from our
customers that they

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.530
run into routes quota.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.370
As somebody approving
quota requests,

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:20.530
we see a lot of increase in
quota requirement because

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.990
of GKE route space clusters.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:25.540
Laying out your VPC is
the most important facet

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.820
of deploying GKE clusters.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.040
One of the key binary
decisions that needs to be made

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.890
is whether you're going to use
routes-based cluster or VPC

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.100
native clusters.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:34.960
Now what are these?

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.890
Kubernetes mandates
port-to-port connectivity.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.350
And when a part is talking
to another part in a node,

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:46.070
it uses the bridge interface
to make that happen.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.270
But across nodes, you need
some support from networking

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:50.230
to make that happen--

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.720
both overlay and underlay.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.810
So GKE started off with
route space cluster.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.600
And what it did was
it used static route.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.550
So it uses static route.

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:03.790
The nodes used a static
route and announced itself

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:07.378
as a next hop to all
the parts that it hosts.

00:03:07.378 --> 00:03:09.670
It's fine for a small cluster,
but as your cluster size

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:12.670
grows, your routes,
quota also increase,

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:15.020
requirements also increase.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:17.950
So in anticipation of growing
clusters, what we did was we

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.150
came up with VPC
native clusters,

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.030
which are under
the hood uses alias

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.540
IP, which basically
is using secondary IP

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.090
on its interface-- on a NIC.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.960
Now VPC native mode
is enabled by default.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.330
And the biggest advantage
over route space cluster

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:38.200
is that it doesn't require
that many routes quota.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.840
Apart from scaling,
as the name suggests,

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.710
it also natively increase
integrates with VPC Fabric,

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:49.750
allowing other functionalities
and features like shared VPC,

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:55.120
private clusters, flexible IP
management, snooping checks,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.600
so on and so forth.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:04.200
So let's talk about
IP management.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.450
Most often the
deployments are blocked

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.000
not because of some
fancy new feature,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.810
but because of lack
of contiguous IPs.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.560
And I've been on many
calls with customers

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.620
where they have a spreadsheet
with IP inventory,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.890
and they're trying to match
the scale of their cluster

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.540
with IPs.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.900
So let's look at IP
management from GKE.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.370
What are some other
resources that use these IPs.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:30.240
And what are some of the
options to reduce IP utilization

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.670
on GKE?

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.610
So Kubernetes itself
uses IPs liberally.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:36.390
Right?

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.100
The different
resources-- so before we

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.410
get into IP
optimization, let's look

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.870
at what other different
resources that uses IP.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:45.270
That are three resources
that require IPs--

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.715
your ports, your nodes,
and your services.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:56.390
So the nodes use the
primary range of a subnet,

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:59.310
the ports use a secondary
range of a subnet,

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:03.450
and the service also uses a
secondary range of a subnet.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:07.600
The service and the port second
range need to be different.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.100
We don't allow you to use
the same secondary range.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.900
Now, what are the advantages of
having separate ranges for all

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.040
these resources?

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.060
There are a few advantages.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.150
The first is it
is more intuitive.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:25.350
If you have any network security
policies and from a tracking

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:29.070
perspective, it's much easier
to have separate ranges.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.835
You can grow each
resource independently.

00:05:31.835 --> 00:05:33.210
And lastly, you
know, if you want

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.790
to apply any security
policy based on CIDRs,

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.610
it's also easier.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:42.850
So VPC native provides two modes
of IP allocation to resources.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:46.230
One is the auto IP mode where
we take care of everything.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:49.740
GKE's responsibility to allocate
IPs to the different resources,

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:53.490
and it takes care of ensuring
there is no IP overlap.

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:57.660
The drawback is that we
assume certain cluster size--

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.850
we assume a moderate
cluster size--

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:04.110
and end up taking an IP block
based on that assumption.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:07.570
However, if you are
building a smaller cluster,

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.600
you know, you want
finer grain control,

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.970
you can use a custom
IP option where

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:15.330
you can decide the subnet,
you can decide the range,

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:18.660
and reduce your
overall IP utilization.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:21.690
One other way of reducing
your IP utilization

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.690
is using the primary
range and the second range

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.940
for ports across
different clusters.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:27.480
Right?

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.695
We don't allow the
secondary range for services

00:06:29.695 --> 00:06:31.320
to be shared across
different clusters,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:37.620
but for the port primary
and for the port secondary

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.462
and for the node
primary, you can share it

00:06:39.462 --> 00:06:40.545
across different clusters.

00:06:43.653 --> 00:06:45.070
And last, but not
the least, there

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:49.100
is a new node that we added
called max ports per node.

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.070
So let's talk about that.

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:53.450
This is feature that
just went into beta.

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:55.600
So to understand the
crux of this feature,

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:58.300
let's define port
density as the number

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.160
of ports running per node.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.410
So we talked about
how you define

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:09.220
a port cluster or a port range,
which allocates IPs to all

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:11.110
the ports in your cluster.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.960
But since the node is
actually running those ports,

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:19.420
the nodes need to carve out
a specific or a smaller CIDR

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:20.560
from this oral cluster.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:21.340
Right?

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:25.520
So with port and
city-based config,

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.660
we have kind of opened up
this option to the customer.

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:32.450
You decide how many ports
you're going to run per node.

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:35.290
And thereby the node carves
out that many eyepiece

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:36.680
from the bigger block.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.910
So as an example, let's say
you give a /24 all your ports

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:44.050
in a cluster, and you're trying
to run eight ports per node,

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:47.320
we take a /28 from
that overall /20.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:51.220
You know, so your /20
exhausts more slowly.

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:54.670
You might ask why are we taking
a /28 if it is only eight

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:56.470
clusters and not /29?

00:07:56.470 --> 00:07:59.830
That's because we take
double the amount of IPs

00:07:59.830 --> 00:08:02.440
for avoiding issues
like IBD reallocation

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:06.980
when the node goes up and down.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:12.400
So this is an example of how you
can create a smaller cluster.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:15.730
Nodes per cluster, 12, so
you take a /28 for that.

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:18.560
Services, you're planning to
run 20 services on this cluster,

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:20.590
so you take a /27 for that.

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:23.720
And the cluster port range
that you define is a /24.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:26.620
Now you define like as we
talked about ports per node.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:31.180
You say every node is going
to have eight ports per node

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:32.500
so that's /28.

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:34.720
And there are 16 /28s.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.090
So you are good from all the
different scaling perspective.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:38.590
Right?

00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:41.830
You have slash /28,
which gives you 16 IPs,

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:48.420
32 IPs for services, and 16
/28, so you're good to go here.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:52.160
So switching tracks from
container native networking

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.760
and from IP management, let's
look at network security.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:58.670
The summary of the ask
is that a customer wants

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:00.680
a completely isolated cluster.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:01.580
Right?

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:04.370
They want to access
Google services privately.

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:07.340
They might have few
workloads, which needs

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:09.980
to access third-party APIs.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:12.080
And for that, they need
a deterministic IP.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:13.940
And they want a
deterministic IP,

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:16.490
because the
third-party vendor has

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:18.320
a whitelist/blacklist
policy, which

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.580
requires a deterministic IP.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:21.080
Right?

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:23.630
So how do you meet
this requirement?

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:26.210
You run something called
GKE private clusters.

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:32.060
And in GKE private clusters,
we take away any public IP,

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:35.000
and it's basically
isolated from the internet.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:37.190
Now I want to
clarify that when I

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:38.930
say it's isolated
from the internet,

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:41.810
you can still configure an
ingress or a service type load

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:45.370
balancer and allow incoming
traffic to the cluster

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:46.120
from the internet.

00:09:48.355 --> 00:09:49.730
So based on this
slide, there are

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:55.180
three steps to take to
isolate your cluster.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:56.650
First is choosing
the cluster type.

00:09:56.650 --> 00:09:59.560
We have primarily a public
or a private cluster.

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:04.030
Depending upon your requirement,
you can choose between the two.

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:06.190
You can expose the
cluster master.

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:08.310
And cluster master
is basically where

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:12.000
we host the [INAUDIBLE] API
server, the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.050
and you know, scheduler as well.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:17.440
So that is in your
control as to how

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:19.090
you're going to expose that.

00:10:19.090 --> 00:10:23.830
And then white listing access
to that cluster master.

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:26.410
Network security is a
shared responsibility

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:29.230
in that it's our
responsibility to secure

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:30.880
the sick the Manage Element.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:33.280
But it's your responsibility
or the user's responsibility

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:36.220
to ensure that they are using
the right option to kind

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:39.970
of lock down the cluster master
as well as the local nodes.

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:42.700
Now in a private cluster,
as I was talking about,

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:44.450
there's only RFC 1918 addresses.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:45.540
There's no public IP.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:46.300
Right?

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:51.480
And this by default prevents it
from talking to the internet.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.850
In private clusters, we turn
on private Google access

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.520
by default, which means you
can access any of the *.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.753
Google APIs privately.

00:10:59.753 --> 00:11:01.170
But if you will
have a requirement

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:02.670
for certain workloads
to go out, you

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:04.470
can also integrate Cloud NAT.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.660
So you can provision a
Cloud NAT and integrate GKE

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:10.350
cluster with the Cloud
NAT functionality.

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:12.750
Second step is exposing the
cluster master endpoint.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.070
So here you have two options.

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:17.160
You can create a
private endpoint, which

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:19.560
gives a private IP to
the cluster master,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.650
and this can be used
to access from on prem.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.480
If you have a kubectl
planned over there,

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.160
you can use that
private endpoint

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:28.210
to access from on prem.

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:29.880
You can also expose
a public endpoint,

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.653
and this is especially useful
for road warrior use cases

00:11:32.653 --> 00:11:34.320
where you have a
remote worker who wants

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.583
to access a cluster master.

00:11:36.583 --> 00:11:38.250
However, you expose
the public endpoint,

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:41.890
it's also accessible to any
untrusted actor as well.

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:44.430
So what we do is we give you
whitelisting, blacklisting--

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:45.930
you know, the white
listing facility

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:49.230
through authorized network
with which you can decide you

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:53.640
can allow the trusted and
disallow the untrusted.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:55.468
So just to summarize--

00:11:55.468 --> 00:11:57.510
I want to talk more about
the authorized network,

00:11:57.510 --> 00:11:58.650
but before that, let
me summarize what

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:00.010
we have discussed thus far.

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:03.060
So there is public endpoint
and private endpoint

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:05.190
for accessing the
cluster master.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:08.160
And in public clusters, you
get only a public endpoint.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.280
In private clusters,
you can have-- you know,

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:12.630
by default, it gives
you a private endpoint,

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:15.900
and it also can extend a
public endpoint [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:17.340
for road warrior use cases.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:22.302
Whitelisting through
the authorized network

00:12:22.302 --> 00:12:24.510
that I was talking about
there is a default behavior,

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:27.768
and there is another
behavior when you enable it.

00:12:27.768 --> 00:12:29.810
I'm not going to go through
go through the table,

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:31.435
but there is a
lot of flexibility

00:12:31.435 --> 00:12:33.560
in terms of different
permutations and combinations

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:36.320
that we support to meet
your security and compliance

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:37.431
requirements.

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:41.370
DNS Scaling.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:46.500
So kube-dns a service that is
used for within the cluster

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.350
name resolution.

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:51.840
Kube-dns deployment logic
takes into account the size

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:55.140
of the cluster, but it doesn't
take into account the number

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:56.190
of requests--

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.320
DNS requests that
might be going across.

00:12:58.320 --> 00:12:58.920
Right?

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:03.410
So the kube-dns lookup
takes up contract entry.

00:13:06.030 --> 00:13:07.440
You might have a use case there.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.860
It's a small enough cluster,
but a very chatty application,

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:12.960
which is exhausting
your contract table

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.480
which can lead to issues.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:19.000
So what you can do
is use DNS autoscaler

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:22.650
to modify the number of
business backends that will run.

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:24.720
And this by default,
the equation

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.020
takes into account the number of
nodes and number of total cores

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:29.017
across the cluster.

00:13:29.017 --> 00:13:30.600
So what you can do
is you can override

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.300
that if you say that, hey,
this is a small cluster.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.970
I'm not satisfied with the
number of default backends

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:38.137
I'm getting, you can increase
the [INAUDIBLE] number

00:13:38.137 --> 00:13:42.510
of backends or you know, DNS
replicas that you want to run.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:46.200
As an example, let's say,
you know, you have 10 nodes,

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:49.870
8 cores per nodes-- so total 80
cores running in the cluster,

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:51.210
so by default, you are getting--

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:52.470
because of the function--

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:55.470
the logic-- you are
getting two DNS replicas,

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:57.480
but because the min
setting that you had,

00:13:57.480 --> 00:13:58.830
you bumped it up to five.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:13:59.430
Right?

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:04.705
So that's another way you can
get over the contract issues.

00:14:08.577 --> 00:14:10.410
As you may know, there
are some applications

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:13.900
that are heavy users of DNS--
you know, node.js an example.

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:16.710
If you have control over the
name that they resolve to,

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:19.335
you can avoid search
path extensions.

00:14:19.335 --> 00:14:21.210
One of them is through
the application itself

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:22.620
that is pointed out in the top.

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:26.250
And the other one is through
making changes to the pod spec.

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:30.240
So with that, I'll
hand it over to Bowei.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:32.340
BOWEI DU: So let's now
talk about running services

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:33.430
at scale.

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:35.120
So at first, you
may start off using

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:37.560
an in-cluster-based
ingress controller, which

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:39.720
just uses an
ordinary proxy server

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:41.850
and uses service type load
balancers to expose it

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:43.040
to the internet.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:45.318
You know this is really
familiar for a lot of us.

00:14:45.318 --> 00:14:46.860
It's just the standard
proxy that you

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:48.453
will run on your
laptop and then you

00:14:48.453 --> 00:14:51.120
would expose your application to
the internet using service type

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:52.410
load balancer.

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:54.360
However, when your
application scales up,

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:57.300
will really want to use the
Google ingress implementation

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:58.980
in Kubernetes engine.

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:00.750
This is a
container-native ingress

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:03.120
that's globally scalable,
low latency, distributed

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:04.140
proxy layer.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:07.010
It has lots of built-in features
like caching, authentication,

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:08.400
DDOS protection.

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:10.560
You won't have to
fiddle with figuring out

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:13.170
how many instances of
your proxy to scale to

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:15.610
or how to worry about
absorbing an attack.

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:18.120
But in addition to this,
what are some best practices

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:20.340
around using services?

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:23.040
So first let's talk
about seamless services.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.010
Production services often
require software updates

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:28.350
and upgrades, and ideally,
without disrupting

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:29.770
to your users.

00:15:29.770 --> 00:15:32.040
Kubernetes engine gives
you a bunch of nods

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:35.010
to ensure that your services
are updated without impact.

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:38.605
But how these pieces
interact can be subtle.

00:15:38.605 --> 00:15:40.230
Let's walk through
each of these pieces

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:41.790
to understand how
they fit together

00:15:41.790 --> 00:15:45.270
to ensure that your users won't
see network hiccups as you

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:48.350
scale and update
your applications.

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:49.170
OK.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:51.960
Let's talk about a pods
lifecycle, particularly

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:56.320
focusing how the pod
lifecycle relates to services.

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:57.900
So there are three
basic states that

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:00.750
govern how your pod
interacts with your services.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:02.370
First, there is liveness.

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:04.410
This determines whether
or not your pod is up.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:07.320
For instance, if your pod
fails, it's liveness check,

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:09.060
it will be restarted.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:11.520
The second bit is readiness.

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:13.680
This determines whether
or not your pod is fully

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:17.250
booted up and healthy and
ready to serve traffic.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:19.710
For instance, this is
used by workload managers

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:22.230
such as a deployment
resource to see

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:25.690
if it's OK to continue
updating your application.

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:27.990
Finally, there
are health checks.

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:30.840
These come from the load
balancer infrastructure itself

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:35.580
in our end-to-end checks of both
network and application health.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:36.330
OK.

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:39.270
So let's walk through what
a pod lifecycle looks like

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:41.920
and how to ensure no
service disruption.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:44.640
So this diagram depicts a
timeline of the pod status.

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:47.500
We have a line for each of
the three states, liveness,

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:50.220
readiness, and health check.

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:51.900
First, we start the pod.

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:52.740
It should be alive.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:55.150
If it wasn't alive
it would be dead.

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:57.720
So then that would be
end of the story OK.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:00.040
So next comes health
check and readiness.

00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:03.790
Now these can go green
independent of each other.

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:06.990
But when they go green, your
pod will start seeing traffic.

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:10.589
The load balancer will be
sending traffic to your pod.

00:17:10.589 --> 00:17:12.599
Now your pod is
running for a while,

00:17:12.599 --> 00:17:15.390
and you do an update
of your deployment.

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:19.380
Here is when your pod is
going to be terminated.

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:23.040
What the system will do is
send your pod a sick term.

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:26.380
If you don't handle it,
your pod simply ends here.

00:17:26.380 --> 00:17:29.620
However, as you can see, it's
still serving some traffic.

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:31.830
So you will drop some
requests on the ground.

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:34.770
If you do handle it,
then what you should do

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:37.500
is start filling the
health check in readiness.

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:40.260
What this will do is
tell the load balancer

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:43.140
to start draining the
traffic off of the pod.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:46.380
Finally, after a
fixed amount of time,

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:49.410
Kubernetes will finally
send the SIGKILL,

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:52.230
which will kill your
application for good.

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:55.440
So how do we have
seamless services?

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:57.060
Let's recap.

00:17:57.060 --> 00:18:00.030
So the most important thing
is when Kubernetes sends you

00:18:00.030 --> 00:18:05.723
a sick term, you have to start
draining your application.

00:18:05.723 --> 00:18:07.890
Requests may still be in
flight to your application,

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:10.110
but no new ones
will arrive there.

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:13.140
Now the way to tell the load
balancer that this is happening

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:16.880
is to fail the health checks
and the readiness probe.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.850
Second thing is after
termination grace period

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:22.795
seconds, your pod will
be killed with SIGKILL.

00:18:22.795 --> 00:18:24.170
Now if you don't
set the setting,

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:25.970
its default is 30 seconds.

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:28.610
But if your application
takes longer than 30 seconds

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:30.530
to service the
last few requests,

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:33.540
make sure you
adjust the setting.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.020
Finally, sending
these grace periods

00:18:36.020 --> 00:18:39.050
may impact how fast a
deployment can update.

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:41.270
So make sure you tune
your deployment mach surge

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:44.550
and max unavailable parameters
to take into account for this.

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:46.640
For instance, if you
can only tolerate

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:49.730
having one pod being
upgraded at a time,

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:52.340
then your upgrade of
your whole deployment

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:54.170
will proceed only
one pod at a time

00:18:54.170 --> 00:18:56.240
and will have to wait
for the grace period.

00:18:59.440 --> 00:18:59.960
Oh, yes.

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:03.320
And one more thing, if
you have infrastructure

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:06.770
that needs to be integrated
into your pod lifecycle,

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:10.530
that does not necessarily
come from your pod itself--

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:13.490
for example, you have to as
part of pod startup, talk

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:16.280
to a network load balancer,
program the load balancer,

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:20.335
and then inform the pod about
the load balancer's condition,

00:19:20.335 --> 00:19:22.460
there's a feature called
pod readiness gates, which

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:25.070
lets you add these custom
conditions from outside

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:28.220
of Kubernetes back into
your pod lifecycle.

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:29.560
So how does this work?

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:32.090
Well, here's your pod lifecycle.

00:19:32.090 --> 00:19:34.880
And we see that the
readiness gates actually

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:37.280
slot in the readiness
condition at the front.

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:43.240
So one concern
that you have when

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:45.400
you scale your
backends to thousands

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:46.990
is that of load balance.

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:49.810
In the ideal case, you
want your request traffic

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:52.660
to be evenly distributed
across your backends.

00:19:52.660 --> 00:19:55.330
So here is a situation
that actually has come up

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:57.970
a few times that we've seen
is suppose I have a load

00:19:57.970 --> 00:20:00.700
balancer with a backend, or
two back ends in this case,

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:02.130
and traffic is flowing.

00:20:02.130 --> 00:20:04.780
And I have traffic
to my backends.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:07.370
And now, I want to
scale up my application.

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:09.150
Now I have four back ends.

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:11.860
And we see that when we send
traffic to these back ends,

00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:16.600
it's actually preferentially
going to the older backends.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:18.370
So why is this?

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:20.840
And is random shuffle
not actually random?

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:22.930
So we know that
Kubernetes and the load

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:25.900
balancer uniformly
randomly sends requests

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:27.120
across all the backends.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:29.110
So what's going on?

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:31.630
To answer this question,
let's zoom in and see what's

00:20:31.630 --> 00:20:32.140
happening.

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:34.150
Let's take a deeper
look to see what

00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:35.542
the infrastructure looks like.

00:20:35.542 --> 00:20:37.000
So at the top, we
have Google Cloud

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:39.070
load balancer and kube-proxy.

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:42.070
Now requests are
balanced to the nodes.

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:43.760
In fact their balance evenly.

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:47.230
But the kube-proxy then balances
connections to the pods.

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:49.150
So if you see here,
we've scaled up.

00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:51.400
We added a few more connections.

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:53.830
But when the requests
come in, traffic

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:56.520
is actually evenly
balanced to the VMs.

00:20:56.520 --> 00:21:00.520
But-- and this is especially
true for newer protocols like

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:02.410
HTTP/2--

00:21:02.410 --> 00:21:04.780
there is a significant
connection reuse

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:06.220
and requests will
actually likely

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:12.170
go to older backends causing
an imbalance of requests.

00:21:12.170 --> 00:21:15.400
So how do we fix this?

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:18.310
So really there is a
disconnect between connections

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:19.540
and requests.

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:20.570
So what to do?

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:23.540
Well, there's two options.

00:21:23.540 --> 00:21:25.340
One of them is complex.

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:28.150
So you can modify the
backend to return headers

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:30.310
indicating the connection
should not be reused.

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:32.800
Basically, the crust
of it is, don't

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:34.210
use the connections too long.

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:37.280
And if you do this
sufficiently often,

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:39.550
you will eventually get
a uniform distribution

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:41.330
of connections.

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:43.180
However, there is
an easier answer,

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:46.212
which is to use
container-native load balancing.

00:21:46.212 --> 00:21:48.670
So with network endpoint point
groups and Google Cloud load

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:52.300
balancer, the load balancer
sees every pod backend

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:54.040
as an independent entity.

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:57.077
In this case, even you have a
very skewed set of connections

00:21:57.077 --> 00:21:58.660
from the load balancer
to the backend,

00:21:58.660 --> 00:22:01.120
the load balancer can look
at that, look at the request

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:05.290
and send it to the
least utilized backend.

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:08.020
And finally, one final
thing is this problem really

00:22:08.020 --> 00:22:11.200
can crop up anytime
you use an L4 service

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:14.440
type with connections,
and you have this kind

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:16.053
of aggressive connection reuse.

00:22:16.053 --> 00:22:17.470
So this is one
thing to watch for.

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:25.730
And this is container of
native load balancing,

00:22:25.730 --> 00:22:29.120
you'll have a nice even
distribution of requests.

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:32.270
Now in addition to
accurate load balancing,

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:35.340
when your LB knows about
your backends directly,

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:37.370
you will have accurate
health checks.

00:22:37.370 --> 00:22:40.310
You won't have the double
hop through kube proxy,

00:22:40.310 --> 00:22:43.040
and you'll have much much
better visibility in debugging.

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:45.870
For example, when you
go dump your traffic,

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:48.410
you'll see the traffic
go directly from the load

00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:50.030
balancer to your backend.

00:22:50.030 --> 00:22:51.450
Nothing in between.

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:56.840
No tracing needed through
kube-proxy or IP tables.

00:22:56.840 --> 00:23:00.590
Now finally, in terms
of scalable services,

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:02.040
we should talk about failures.

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:04.850
So the common way to
think about failures

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:06.800
is to think about
failure domains.

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:09.530
And in this case, we have
to think both about our data

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:11.390
and control plane.

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:13.700
What this means is that if
you have a single or maybe

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:15.770
multiple faults, they
won't cause disruptions

00:23:15.770 --> 00:23:18.000
to your users.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:23.260
Now, we could have failures
of, for example, a VM.

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:25.010
And if you have a
correlated failure where

00:23:25.010 --> 00:23:26.630
all of your pods
of your application

00:23:26.630 --> 00:23:30.290
are scheduled on the same VM,
then you may have an outage.

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:33.450
One way to avoid this is to
use scheduling constraints,

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:36.440
such as pod anti-affinity
to distribute

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:40.610
your pods across the
nodes in your cluster.

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:42.830
Another failure domain
you want to consider

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:44.180
is a zone old failure.

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:48.770
For example, in this case,
US West 1A has gone down.

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:52.670
To handle zonal failures, you'd
probably should do two things.

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:54.380
One for your data
plane, you should

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:57.830
have node pools that are
spread across multiple zones.

00:23:57.830 --> 00:23:59.330
And for your control
plane, you will

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:01.970
want to use regional clusters.

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:06.860
Regional clusters gives you
a distributed control plane

00:24:06.860 --> 00:24:11.570
across multiple zones
for your API servers.

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:17.580
Finally, we forgot
about one thing,

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:19.380
which is the config plane.

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:22.393
So not only do you have the data
plane and the control plane,

00:24:22.393 --> 00:24:24.060
but you should be
careful that you can't

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:25.650
make that one edit to a config.

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:29.830
I know we all did it, and it
takes down your global network.

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:34.350
So ideally, you should have
separation here as well.

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:37.470
Separate your clusters by
namespaces or by clusters

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:39.270
for staging and production.

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:41.490
Make sure you stage a copy
of Kubernetes resources,

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:43.600
test, and then commit
the real thing.

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:45.440
So that's the
advantage of Kubernetes

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:48.450
is this declarative model
that can be checked in,

00:24:48.450 --> 00:24:51.030
and you can run
CI/CD on your YAMLs.

00:24:51.030 --> 00:24:53.550
In addition to thinking
about it at the sort

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:56.790
of cluster/namespace level,
you can also think about it

00:24:56.790 --> 00:24:58.420
at the services level.

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:01.230
For example, if
you have a service,

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:04.080
you can use the Kubernetes
selectors to merge

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:05.760
multiple deployments together.

00:25:05.760 --> 00:25:07.710
So you don't have that
one golden deployment

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:12.280
that you can edit and put
all your eggs in that basket.

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:12.780
OK.

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:16.035
So we've seen a couple
of best practices.

00:25:16.035 --> 00:25:17.910
And now let's go over
what they actually look

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:20.820
like on a Kubernetes engine.

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:23.290
So first, we're
creating a cluster.

00:25:23.290 --> 00:25:26.470
And as we recall, we want
multiple failure domains.

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:29.100
So we create a regional cluster.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:32.010
Next, we know that we
want IP flexibility

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:34.740
in all the access all
the features, so we make

00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:36.780
sure it's a VPC-native cluster.

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:41.340
And also we have sort of
know how our workload scales

00:25:41.340 --> 00:25:43.740
with respect to
IPs, so we can set

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:47.070
a smaller maximum pods
per node to kind of save

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:49.500
on IP address usage.

00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:52.080
Finally, we use
HTTP load balancing,

00:25:52.080 --> 00:25:54.480
which is the Google ingress.

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:56.220
And we want a private
cluster, because we

00:25:56.220 --> 00:25:57.730
want it to be secure.

00:25:57.730 --> 00:26:01.930
And finally, we put ACLs using
master authorized networks.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:05.460
So let's see what this looks
like when we make the cluster.

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:06.210
Can we switch?

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:13.900
OK.

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:15.560
So we have this cluster.

00:26:15.560 --> 00:26:16.060
Right?

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:18.070
And we see that it
has three nodes.

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:19.840
In fact, all of
these three nodes

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:24.170
run across the
distributed control plane,

00:26:24.170 --> 00:26:26.920
which is in a region US West 1.

00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:29.260
And in fact, when we
look at our nodes,

00:26:29.260 --> 00:26:33.820
we see that they use less
space than the default. Right?

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:35.320
Because we have
scaled our cluster

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:38.230
to be smaller IPs
per node, so we

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:41.560
can fit more nodes
into our network.

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:43.610
And also from the failure
domain perspective,

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:47.170
we see that it's actually
sharded across all of US1.

00:26:47.170 --> 00:26:52.480
So if one of these zonal shards
goes down, we still are up.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:55.450
Now let's look at the
container-native load balancer.

00:26:55.450 --> 00:27:00.550
So this is a standard definition
for an application with a load

00:27:00.550 --> 00:27:01.100
balancer.

00:27:01.100 --> 00:27:03.550
We have the
deployment, which I was

00:27:03.550 --> 00:27:05.500
speaking about earlier
with how you get

00:27:05.500 --> 00:27:07.730
your workload in the cluster.

00:27:07.730 --> 00:27:10.090
Then we have a service,
and the services

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:15.340
where we say we want to use
network endpoint groups,

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:18.390
which is the container-native
load balancing.

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:23.890
And finally, we expose the
application using ingress here.

00:27:23.890 --> 00:27:30.740
Now, let's take a look at what
this load balancer looks like.

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:32.230
So here we have
the load balancer

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:36.790
that was created from that
snippet of configuration.

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:41.330
And you'll see that it's
split over three zones.

00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:41.830
Right?

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:44.920
We have three zones
for reliability.

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:47.650
And in fact, when we
click into the zone,

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:53.370
we'll see that all of
my backends are my pods.

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:55.890
So I have very
accurate health checks

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:58.860
from the infrastructure
telling me

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:00.660
whether that
infrastructure, the load

00:28:00.660 --> 00:28:05.140
balancer to that particular
pod directly is healthy or not.

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:05.640
All right.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:09.540
So in summary, what were some
of the best practices for using

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:12.640
GKE in regards to networking?

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:16.500
Well, first, you should sit down
and understand your cluster IP

00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:21.420
space and plan for
future growth and also

00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:24.990
use VPC native clusters to
get the maximum flexibility

00:28:24.990 --> 00:28:27.130
in terms of IP space planning.

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:30.630
The second thing is make
sure you understand what

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:32.620
your security requirements are.

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:35.280
But really, you should
be using private cluster

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:39.390
and master authorized networks
to secure your clusters.

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:43.980
Also tune your pod lifecycle
to avoid service disruption.

00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:45.730
You know, handle sig term.

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:49.020
Make sure you respond correctly
to the readiness and health

00:28:49.020 --> 00:28:50.820
checks.

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:52.590
Use container-native
load balancing

00:28:52.590 --> 00:28:55.710
for the best performance and
visibility of your traffic.

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:58.920
And finally, understand
and plan for failures

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.150
on the three planes--

00:29:00.150 --> 00:29:02.952
data, control, and config.

00:29:02.952 --> 00:29:04.660
And your feedback is
greatly appreciated,

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:07.103
so please fill out the
survey app at the end.

